> Module Qualifying_relative

> Using Params_2024 as P
> Using Qualifying_child as Q

# Qualifying relative and eligible dependent: IRC 152(d), 152(b), one person at a time

Direct File ("DF" below) facts `/familyAndHousehold/*/qualifyingRelative`,
`/familyAndHousehold/*/eligibleDependent`, `/familyAndHousehold/*/isClaimedDependent`,
`/familyAndHousehold/*/isClaimedIfClaimable` and their sub-tests in
`familyAndHousehold.xml` (lines 2406-2448, 2549-3040, 3423-3439) and
`/familyAndHousehold/*/qrRelationshipTest` in `dependentsRelationship.xml`
(lines 1110-1260), under `../direct-file/direct-file/backend/src/main/resources/tax/`.
Issue #17, scope.md section 5.2. Tests:
`catala/tests/test_qualifying_relative.catala_en`. Oracle comparison:
`difftest/mappings/qualifying_relative.mjs` against the per-person oracle
`oracle/persons.csv` (`make difftest-persons`).

The file is named after the topic, as `qualifying_child.catala_en` (#16) is,
not after the DF XML files (CONVENTIONS section 9 lists
`family_and_household.catala_en` and `dependents_relationship.catala_en` for
#16 and #17 together; #16 set the per-topic precedent and this module follows it).

Like `Qualifying_child`, this module works on one member of the DF collection
`/familyAndHousehold` at a time (scope `DependentTest`) and maps that scope over
the household (scope `Dependents`). It calls `Qualifying_child.QualifyingChildTest`
for the same person: a qualifying relative must not be a qualifying child, a
dependent is either, and the joint return test is shared.

Facts read from outside the person, all inputs here:

- `/minimumFilerDateOfBirth` (`Filers.minimum_filer_date_of_birth`, #15), for
  the qualifying child test;
- `/treatFilersAsDependents` (`Filers.treat_filers_as_dependents`, #15),
  IRC 152(b)(1);
- `/eitherFilerUSCitizenOrNational` (`Filers.either_filer_us_citizen_or_national`,
  #15), IRC 152(b)(3)(B)(ii);
- per person, `dependentsBenefitSplit.xml` `/familyAndHousehold/*/tpCanClaimSplitBenefitsHohEitcCdcc`
  and `/familyAndHousehold/*/disqualifiedDueToParentalCustody` (IRC 152(e),
  issue #18, not yet written). They are fields of `DependentAnswers` until #18
  provides them. TODO(#18): take them from the benefit-split scope.

The inputs are complete (CONVENTIONS section 3). Where DF's question gates
disagree with the statute, the statute wins (CONVENTIONS section 8,
`# DF-BUG(n):` comments; two cases below, `DF-BUG(15)` and `DF-BUG(16)`).

Not here, on purpose: the children of divorced or separated parents and
multiple support agreements (IRC 152(e), 152(d)(3), issue #18 and the
`writableQrSupportTest` answer), HoH and QSS qualifying persons (#19, #20),
the credit-specific person tests (#26-#28). This module feeds those modules
and Form 1040 "Dependents" (the list of claimed dependents); it feeds no Form
1040 dollar line directly.

## Declarations

```catala-metadata
## DF: constants.xml /tinTypeOptions (lines 786-797), same order. The type of
## the person's taxpayer identification number; `NoTin` is DF `none` (renamed:
## `None` reads as an optional value).
declaration enumeration TinType:
  -- Ssn
  -- Itin
  -- Atin
  -- NoTin

## The `Writable` answers DF keeps per household member that the qualifying
## relative and dependent tests read, beyond those of `Qualifying_child.Person`.
declaration structure DependentAnswers:
  # The person's gross income for 2024 (IRC 152(d)(1)(B)). DF asks the yes/no
  # question /familyAndHousehold/*/grossIncomeTest ("Was <name>'s gross income
  # less than $5,050?"); here the amount is the input and the comparison is a
  # rule. The difftest mapping turns DF's answer into $0 (yes) or the limit (no).
  data gross_income content money
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/writableQrSupportTest ("you provided more than
  # half their support")
  data writable_qr_support_test content boolean
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/tinType
  data tin_type content TinType
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/isUsCitizenFullYear (UI: "a U.S. citizen at any
  # time in 2024?")
  data is_us_citizen_full_year content boolean
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/writableUsResident
  data writable_us_resident content boolean
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/writableUsNational
  data writable_us_national content boolean
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/writableCanadaMexicoResident
  data writable_canada_mexico_resident content boolean
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/tpClaims ("Whether the taxpayer wants to claim
  # the dependent")
  data tp_claims content boolean
  # DF: dependentsBenefitSplit.xml /familyAndHousehold/*/tpCanClaimSplitBenefitsHohEitcCdcc
  # (derived there, issue #18; an input until #18 lands)
  data tp_can_claim_split_benefits_hoh_eitc_cdcc content boolean
  # DF: dependentsBenefitSplit.xml /familyAndHousehold/*/disqualifiedDueToParentalCustody
  # (derived there, issue #18; an input until #18 lands)
  data disqualified_due_to_parental_custody content boolean

## One member of DF /familyAndHousehold: the qualifying-child answers and the
## dependent answers.
declaration structure Member:
  data person content Q.Person
  data answers content DependentAnswers
```

## The scope for one person

```catala-metadata
declaration scope DependentTest:
  # One member of DF /familyAndHousehold
  input member content Member
  # DF: filers.xml /minimumFilerDateOfBirth (Filers.minimum_filer_date_of_birth)
  input minimum_filer_date_of_birth content date
  # DF: filers.xml /treatFilersAsDependents (Filers.treat_filers_as_dependents)
  input treat_filers_as_dependents content boolean
  # DF: filers.xml /eitherFilerUSCitizenOrNational
  # (Filers.either_filer_us_citizen_or_national)
  input either_filer_us_citizen_or_national content boolean

  # The qualifying child tests for the same person (issue #16)
  output qc content Q.QualifyingChildTest

  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/qualifyingChild (copied from `qc`)
  output qualifying_child condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/askAboutQCOfAnother (DF's question gate; kept
  # for comparison, not used by the rules below: DF-BUG(16))
  output ask_about_qc_of_another condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/isQualifyingChildOfAnother (DF's value, with the
  # gate; kept for comparison and for the HoH/EITC modules)
  output is_qualifying_child_of_another condition
  # The person passes the parts of IRC 152(c) that do not depend on the
  # taxpayer (no DF fact; DF-BUG(16))
  output could_be_qualifying_child_of_some_taxpayer condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/notQualifyingChildTest
  output not_qualifying_child_test condition
  # DF: dependentsRelationship.xml /familyAndHousehold/*/qrRelationshipTest
  output qr_relationship_test condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/memberOfHouseholdTest
  output member_of_household_test condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/relationshipMemberOfHouseholdTest
  output relationship_member_of_household_test condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/grossIncomeTest (a Writable in DF; see
  # DependentAnswers.gross_income)
  output gross_income_test condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/qrSupportTest
  output qr_support_test condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/qualifyingRelativeMinusNotQcTest
  output qualifying_relative_minus_not_qc_test condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/qualifyingRelative
  output qualifying_relative condition

  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/usResident
  output us_resident condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/usNational
  output us_national condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/canadaMexicoResident
  output canada_mexico_resident condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/childIsAdopted
  output child_is_adopted condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/adoptedChildException
  output adopted_child_exception condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/citizenOrUsResidentTest (CTC/ODC, issue #27)
  output citizen_or_us_resident_test condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/citizenOrResidentTest
  output citizen_or_resident_test condition

  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/qcOrQr
  output qc_or_qr condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/eligibleDependentMinusFilerInformation
  output eligible_dependent_minus_filer_information condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/eligibleDependent
  output eligible_dependent condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/isClaimedDependent
  output is_claimed_dependent condition
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/isClaimedIfClaimable
  output is_claimed_if_claimable condition
```

```catala
scope DependentTest:
  definition qc equals
    output of Q.QualifyingChildTest with {
      -- person: member.person
      -- minimum_filer_date_of_birth: minimum_filer_date_of_birth }

  # IRC 152(c). DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/qualifyingChild
  rule qualifying_child under condition qc.qualifying_child
  consequence fulfilled
```

## 26 U.S.C. § 152(a) Dependent

IRC 152(a) (`sources/statute/irc-152.html`): "For purposes of this subtitle,
the term "dependent" means— (1) a qualifying child, or (2) a qualifying
relative." Pub. 501 (2024), `sources/pubs/p501--2024.pdf` p 11: "The term
"dependent" means: • A qualifying child, or • A qualifying relative." DF
`/familyAndHousehold/*/qcOrQr` (`familyAndHousehold.xml` line 3004).

```catala
scope DependentTest:
  # IRC 152(a); Pub 501 p 11. DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/qcOrQr
  rule qc_or_qr under condition qualifying_child or qualifying_relative
  consequence fulfilled
```

## 26 U.S.C. § 152(d) Qualifying relative

### (1) In general

IRC 152(d)(1): "The term "qualifying relative" means, with respect to any
taxpayer for any taxable year, an individual— (A) who bears a relationship to
the taxpayer described in paragraph (2), (B) whose gross income for the
calendar year in which such taxable year begins is less than the exemption
amount (as defined in section 151(d)), (C) with respect to whom the taxpayer
provides over one-half of the individual's support for the calendar year in
which such taxable year begins, and (D) who is not a qualifying child of such
taxpayer or of any other taxpayer for any taxable year beginning in the
calendar year in which such taxable year begins."

Pub. 501 p 18, Qualifying Relative: "Four tests must be met for a person to be
your qualifying relative. The four tests are: 1. Not a qualifying child test,
2. Member of household or relationship test, 3. Gross income test, and 4.
Support test." "Unlike a qualifying child, a qualifying relative can be any
age. There is no age test for a qualifying relative."

DF `/familyAndHousehold/*/qualifyingRelative` (line 2750) is the `All` of
`notQualifyingChildTest` and `qualifyingRelativeMinusNotQcTest` (line 2763, the
other three tests).

**The person must not be the filer's own qualifying child.** 152(d)(1)(D)
says "not a qualifying child of such taxpayer or of any other taxpayer"; Pub.
501 p 18: "A child isn't your qualifying relative if the child is your
qualifying child or the qualifying child of any other taxpayer. Example 1.
Your 22-year-old child, who is a student, lives with you and meets all the
tests to be your qualifying child. This child isn't your qualifying relative."
DF's `notQualifyingChildTest` only tests "of any other taxpayer", so DF
calls a qualifying child of the filer who also passes the other three tests a
qualifying relative. That changes no dependent (`qcOrQr` is true either way)
but the HoH rules read `qualifyingRelative` directly (`hohQualifyingPerson`,
lines 3390 and 3404). We follow the statute: `DF-BUG(15)`. The oracle shows
it on two persons (a 4-year-old child in `hoh-cdcc-combat-pay` and
`hoh-cdcc-multiple-careproviders`: DF `qualifyingRelative` true, here false).
Those children pass DF's qualifying child branch of `hohQualifyingPerson` as
well, so the filing status does not change; issue #19 should confirm that the
qualifying relative branch never admits one of the filer's qualifying
children that the qualifying child branch rejects.

```catala
scope DependentTest:
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/qualifyingRelativeMinusNotQcTest
  rule qualifying_relative_minus_not_qc_test under condition
    relationship_member_of_household_test and gross_income_test
    and qr_support_test
  consequence fulfilled

  # DF-BUG(15): DF's /qualifyingRelative omits "not a qualifying child of
  # such taxpayer" (IRC 152(d)(1)(D); Pub 501 p 18, Example 1). We add it.
  # Status: candidate. Oracle: 2 persons differ on this fact (text above).
  # IRC 152(d)(1); Pub 501 p 18. DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/qualifyingRelative
  rule qualifying_relative under condition
    not qualifying_child
    and not_qualifying_child_test
    and qualifying_relative_minus_not_qc_test
  consequence fulfilled
```

### (1)(D) Not the qualifying child of any other taxpayer

Pub. 501 p 18, Not a Qualifying Child Test, "Example 2. Your 2-year-old
child lives with your parents and meets all the tests to be their qualifying
child. This child isn't your qualifying relative." And: "Child of person not
required to file a return. A child isn't the qualifying child of any other
taxpayer and so may qualify as your qualifying relative if the child's parent
(or other person for whom the child is defined as a qualifying child) isn't
required to file an income tax return and either: • Doesn't file an income
tax return, or • Files a return only to get a refund of income tax withheld
or estimated tax paid."

DF asks "Is the dependent the qualifying child of any other taxpayer?"
(`writableCouldBeQualifyingChildOfAnother`, line 2450) and the three
questions about that taxpayer's filing (lines 2483-2547), combined in
`qcOfAnotherButNot` (line 2549) exactly as Pub. 501 says. The
`Qualifying_child` module computes the result as
`other_taxpayer_can_claim_as_qualifying_child`: the answer is yes, and the
other taxpayer is not the non-filer / refund-only case.

**The question gate.** DF asks the question only when `askAboutQCOfAnother`
(line 2418): the person passes DF's `ageTest` and either the other three
qualifying relative tests or the qualifying child tests, and is not in a
benefit split. When the question is not asked DF takes the answer as no
(`couldntBeQCOfAnother`, line 2406), with the comment "If the person doesn't
meet the age test, they couldn't be anyone's QC". That is not so: DF's
`ageTest` includes "younger than the taxpayer claiming such individual"
(IRC 152(c)(3)(A)), measured against *this* filer. A 21-year-old full-time
student who lives with a 20-year-old sibling fails the age test for the
sibling, but can be the qualifying child of a parent, and is then not the
sibling's qualifying relative (152(d)(1)(D)). The other half of the gate
(ask only if the other three qualifying relative tests or the qualifying
child tests pass) is harmless for `qualifyingRelative`, which fails anyway,
but `notQualifyingChildTest` is also read alone by the QSS rule
(`qssQualifyingPerson`, line 3488, with `qrSupportTest`), and IRC 2(a)(1)(B)
determines that dependent "without regard to subsections (b)(1), (b)(2), and
(d)(1)(B)", the gross income test among them. A person who fails only the
gross income test is never asked, and DF then counts them as nobody else's
qualifying child.

What we do instead: use the answer, but only for a person who can be anyone's
qualifying child at all, i.e. who passes the parts of IRC 152(c) that do not
depend on who the taxpayer is: the support test (152(c)(1)(D)), the joint
return test (152(c)(1)(E)), and the age limits without "younger than the
taxpayer" (152(c)(3): under 19, or a student under 24, or permanently and
totally disabled). A "yes" for anybody else contradicts the statute and is
ignored. This is `DF-BUG(16)`. The DF valueThe DF value
`isQualifyingChildOfAnother` (line 2565) is still computed, as the output
`is_qualifying_child_of_another`, because the HoH and EITC rules (issues #19,
#26) read it.

```catala
scope DependentTest:
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/askAboutQCOfAnother (complete inputs, so the
  # IsComplete test is true)
  rule ask_about_qc_of_another under condition
    qc.age_test
    and (qualifying_relative_minus_not_qc_test or qualifying_child)
    and not member.answers.tp_can_claim_split_benefits_hoh_eitc_cdcc
  consequence fulfilled

  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/isQualifyingChildOfAnother. couldBeQualifying
  # ChildOfAnother is the answer behind the gate.
  rule is_qualifying_child_of_another under condition
    ask_about_qc_of_another
    and member.person.writable_could_be_qualifying_child_of_another
    and (qualifying_child or not qc.qc_of_another_but_not)
  consequence fulfilled

  # DF-BUG(16): DF reads the "qualifying child of another taxpayer" answer
  # only behind askAboutQCOfAnother, which requires the person to pass the age
  # test for this filer ("younger than the taxpayer", IRC 152(c)(3)(A)) and
  # the other qualifying relative or qualifying child tests. The other
  # taxpayer's age test is not this filer's, and the QSS rule reads this fact
  # without the gross income test (IRC 2(a)(1)(B)). IRC 152(d)(1)(D); Pub 501
  # p 18. Status: candidate. We use the answer for every person who can be
  # anyone's qualifying child.
  # IRC 152(d)(1)(D); Pub 501 p 18. DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/notQualifyingChildTest
  rule not_qualifying_child_test under condition
    not (could_be_qualifying_child_of_some_taxpayer
         and qc.other_taxpayer_can_claim_as_qualifying_child)
  consequence fulfilled

  # IRC 152(c)(1)(D), (E), (3) without "younger than the taxpayer"; no DF fact
  # (DF-BUG(16), text above)
  rule could_be_qualifying_child_of_some_taxpayer under condition
    qc.qc_support_test
    and qc.joint_return_test
    and (qc.age < Q.qc_age_limit
         or (member.person.full_time_student and qc.age < Q.qc_student_age_limit)
         or member.person.permanent_total_disability)
  consequence fulfilled
```

### (1)(A) and (2) Relationship or member of household

IRC 152(d)(2): "an individual bears a relationship to the taxpayer described
in this paragraph if the individual is any of the following with respect to
the taxpayer: (A) A child or a descendant of a child. (B) A brother, sister,
stepbrother, or stepsister. (C) The father or mother, or an ancestor of
either. (D) A stepfather or stepmother. (E) A son or daughter of a brother or
sister of the taxpayer. (F) A brother or sister of the father or mother of
the taxpayer. (G) A son-in-law, daughter-in-law, father-in-law,
mother-in-law, brother-in-law, or sister-in-law. (H) An individual (other
than an individual who at any time during the taxable year was the spouse,
determined without regard to section 7703, of the taxpayer) who, for the
taxable year of the taxpayer, has the same principal place of abode as the
taxpayer and is a member of the taxpayer's household."

IRC 152(f)(1)(A): "The term "child" means an individual who is— (i) a son,
daughter, stepson, or stepdaughter of the taxpayer, or (ii) an eligible
foster child of the taxpayer." 152(f)(1)(B): "a legally adopted individual of
the taxpayer, or an individual who is lawfully placed with the taxpayer for
legal adoption by the taxpayer, shall be treated as a child of such
individual by blood." 152(f)(4): "The terms "brother" and "sister" include a
brother or sister by the half blood."

Pub. 501 pp 18-19, Relatives who don't have to live with you: "• Your child,
stepchild, or foster child, or a descendant of any of them (for example, your
grandchild). (A legally adopted child is considered your child.) • Your
brother, sister, half brother, half sister, stepbrother, or stepsister. •
Your father, mother, grandparent, or other direct ancestor, but not foster
parent. • Your stepfather or stepmother. • A son or daughter of your brother
or sister. • A son or daughter of your half brother or half sister. • A
brother or sister of your father or mother. • Your son-in-law,
daughter-in-law, father-in-law, mother-in-law, brother-in-law, or
sister-in-law." Pub. 501 p 19: "Joint return. If you file a joint return,
the person can be related to either you or your spouse." "Cousin. Your cousin
must live with you all year as a member of your household to meet this test."

DF `qrRelationshipTest` (`dependentsRelationship.xml` line 1110) lists the 19
options below: (A) the five child options, (B) `sibling`, `halfSibling`,
`stepSibling`, (C) `parent`, `grandParent`, `otherAncestorOfParent`, (D)
`stepParent`, (E) `childOfSibling`, `childOfHalfSibling`, (F)
`siblingOfParent`, (G) `childInLaw`, `parentInLaw`, `siblingInLaw`,
`siblingsSpouse` (a brother-in-law or sister-in-law by the sibling's
marriage). Correctly absent: `fosterParent` ("but not foster parent"), the
child of a step-sibling and further descendants of siblings (152(d)(2)(E)
names only a "son or daughter of a brother or sister"), and
`noneOfTheAbove`. Those pass only under (H).

(H) is DF `memberOfHouseholdTest` (line 2635): the person lived with the
filer `allYear`. Pub. 501 p 19 counts as living with you the temporary
absences, a person who died during the year or was born during the year and
lived with you for the rest of it, and an adopted or foster child placed
during the year; DF folds those into the `allYear` answer. The spouse
exclusion of (H) and the local-law rule of 152(f)(3) have no DF question; the
filer's spouse is never a member of `/familyAndHousehold`.

```catala
scope DependentTest:
  # IRC 152(d)(2)(A)-(G), 152(f)(1), 152(f)(4); Pub 501 pp 18-19.
  # DF: dependentsRelationship.xml /familyAndHousehold/*/qrRelationshipTest
  rule qr_relationship_test under condition
    match member.person.relationship with pattern
    -- Q.Relationship.BiologicalChild: true
    -- Q.Relationship.AdoptedChild: true
    -- Q.Relationship.StepChild: true
    -- Q.Relationship.FosterChild: true
    -- Q.Relationship.GrandChildOrOtherDescendantOfChild: true
    -- Q.Relationship.Sibling: true
    -- Q.Relationship.HalfSibling: true
    -- Q.Relationship.StepSibling: true
    -- Q.Relationship.Parent: true
    -- Q.Relationship.GrandParent: true
    -- Q.Relationship.OtherAncestorOfParent: true
    -- Q.Relationship.StepParent: true
    -- Q.Relationship.ChildOfSibling: true
    -- Q.Relationship.ChildOfHalfSibling: true
    -- Q.Relationship.SiblingOfParent: true
    -- Q.Relationship.ChildInLaw: true
    -- Q.Relationship.ParentInLaw: true
    -- Q.Relationship.SiblingInLaw: true
    -- Q.Relationship.SiblingsSpouse: true
    -- anything: false
  consequence fulfilled

  # IRC 152(d)(2)(H); Pub 501 p 19. DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/memberOfHouseholdTest
  rule member_of_household_test under condition
    member.person.residency_duration = Q.ResidencyDuration.AllYear
  consequence fulfilled

  # IRC 152(d)(1)(A), (2); Pub 501 p 18.
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/relationshipMemberOfHouseholdTest
  rule relationship_member_of_household_test under condition
    qr_relationship_test or member_of_household_test
  consequence fulfilled
```

### (1)(B) Gross income

IRC 152(d)(1)(B): "whose gross income for the calendar year in which such
taxable year begins is less than the exemption amount (as defined in section
151(d))". IRC 151(d)(5)(A) makes the exemption amount zero for 2018 through
2025, but 151(d)(5)(B): "the reduction of the exemption amount to zero under
subparagraph (A) shall not be taken into account in determining whether a
deduction is allowed or allowable, or whether a taxpayer is entitled to a
deduction, under this section." Rev. Proc. 2023-34 §3.24
(`sources/revproc/rp-23-34.pdf` p 14): "For taxable years beginning in 2024,
the exemption amount referenced in § 152(d)(1)(B) is $5,050." Pub. 501 p 19,
Gross Income Test: "To meet this test, a person's gross income for the year
must be less than $5,050."

DF `/grossIncomeLimit` (line 2664) is $5,050; its description cites "Rev Proc
.24", which is §3.24 of Rev. Proc. 2023-34 (`sources/VERIFICATION.md`), not
Rev. Proc. 2024-40 (whose figure, $5,200, is for 2025). The constant is
`Params_2024.qualifying_relative_gross_income_limit`. DF's
`grossIncomeTest` is the filer's yes/no answer to "Was <name>'s gross income
less than $5,050?"; here the amount is the input and "less than" is the rule,
so $5,049 passes and $5,050 fails. The sheltered-workshop exclusion of
152(d)(4) is part of figuring the amount, as in DF.

```catala
scope DependentTest:
  # IRC 152(d)(1)(B), 151(d)(5)(B); Rev. Proc. 2023-34 §3.24, rp-23-34.pdf p 14;
  # Pub 501 p 19. DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/grossIncomeTest, /grossIncomeLimit
  rule gross_income_test under condition
    member.answers.gross_income < P.qualifying_relative_gross_income_limit
  consequence fulfilled
```

### (1)(C) Support

IRC 152(d)(1)(C): "with respect to whom the taxpayer provides over one-half of
the individual's support for the calendar year in which such taxable year
begins". Pub. 501 p 19, Support Test (To Be a Qualifying Relative): "To meet
this test, you must generally provide more than half of a person's total
support during the calendar year."

DF `/familyAndHousehold/*/qrSupportTest` (line 2718): false if the person
provided over half of their own support (`ownSupport`, also asked for the
qualifying child support test), else the answer `writableQrSupportTest`. A
person who pays over half of their own support cannot receive over half from
the filer, so the first case is the statute too. Multiple support agreements
(152(d)(3)) and the divorced-parents rule (152(e)) are part of answering
`writableQrSupportTest` (issue #18 for 152(e)).

```catala
scope DependentTest:
  # IRC 152(d)(1)(C); Pub 501 p 19. DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/qrSupportTest
  rule qr_support_test under condition
    not member.person.own_support and member.answers.writable_qr_support_test
  consequence fulfilled
```

## 26 U.S.C. § 152(b) Exceptions

Pub. 501 p 11, Exceptions: "Even if you have a qualifying child or qualifying
relative, you can claim that person as a dependent only if these three tests
are met. 1. Dependent taxpayer test. 2. Joint return test. 3. Citizen or
resident test."

### (1) Dependents ineligible

IRC 152(b)(1): "If an individual is a dependent of a taxpayer for any taxable
year of such taxpayer beginning in a calendar year, such individual shall be
treated as having no dependents for any taxable year of such individual
beginning in such calendar year." Pub. 501 p 11, Dependent Taxpayer Test: "If
you can be claimed as a dependent by another taxpayer, you can't claim anyone
else as a dependent." "Exception. ... if the person who can claim you (or
your spouse if filing a joint return) as a dependent files a return only to
claim a refund of income tax withheld or estimated tax paid." DF
`/treatFilersAsDependents` (`filers.xml` line 1441, computed by `Filers`)
holds that test, refund exception included.

### (2) Married dependents

IRC 152(b)(2): "An individual shall not be treated as a dependent of a
taxpayer under subsection (a) if such individual has made a joint return with
the individual's spouse under section 6013 for the taxable year beginning in
the calendar year in which the taxable year of the taxpayer begins." Pub. 501
p 11, Joint Return Test: "You generally can't claim a married person as a
dependent if that person files a joint return." "Exception. You can claim a
person as a dependent who files a joint return if that person and that
person's spouse file the joint return only to claim a refund of income tax
withheld or estimated tax paid." DF reuses the qualifying child's
`jointReturnTest` (computed by `Qualifying_child`, which has this exception).

### (3) Citizens or nationals of other countries

IRC 152(b)(3)(A): "The term "dependent" does not include an individual who is
not a citizen or national of the United States unless such individual is a
resident of the United States or a country contiguous to the United States."
Treas. Reg. § 1.152-2(a)(1) (`sources/regs/cfr-1.152-2.xml`): "a citizen or
resident of the United States ... Canada, or Mexico, or ... a national of the
United States, at some time during the calendar year". Pub. 501 p 11,
Citizen or Resident Test: "You generally can't claim a person as a dependent
unless that person is a U.S. citizen, a U.S. resident alien, a U.S. national,
or a resident of Canada or Mexico."

DF asks in order: citizen at any time in 2024 (`isUsCitizenFullYear`, whose
UI text is "a U.S. citizen at any time in 2024?"), if not then U.S. resident,
if not then U.S. national, if not then resident of Canada or Mexico; each
derived fact is the answer ANDed with "not" of the earlier ones (lines
2812-2865).

IRC 152(b)(3)(B): "Subparagraph (A) shall not exclude any child of a taxpayer
(within the meaning of subsection (f)(1)(B)) from the definition of
"dependent" if— (i) for the taxable year of the taxpayer, the child has the
same principal place of abode as the taxpayer and is a member of the
taxpayer's household, and (ii) the taxpayer is a citizen or national of the
United States." Treas. Reg. § 1.152-2(a)(2)(i): "his legally adopted child
who lives with him, as a member of his household, for the entire taxable
year". Pub. 501 p 11, Exception for adopted child: "If you are a U.S. citizen
or U.S. national who has legally adopted a child who isn't a U.S. citizen,
U.S. resident alien, or U.S. national, this test is met if the child lived
with you as a member of your household all year." DF
`adoptedChildException` (line 2908): relationship `adoptedChild`, either
filer a citizen or national (on a joint return, either spouse is "the
taxpayer"), and residency `allYear`.

DF `citizenOrUsResidentTest` (line 2943) is the same test without the Canada
and Mexico residents; the CTC and ODC read it (issue #27).

```catala
scope DependentTest:
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/usResident
  rule us_resident under condition
    not member.answers.is_us_citizen_full_year
    and member.answers.writable_us_resident
  consequence fulfilled

  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/usNational
  rule us_national under condition
    not member.answers.is_us_citizen_full_year
    and not us_resident
    and member.answers.writable_us_national
  consequence fulfilled

  # IRC 152(b)(3)(A) "a country contiguous to the United States"; Pub 501 p 11.
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/canadaMexicoResident
  rule canada_mexico_resident under condition
    not member.answers.is_us_citizen_full_year
    and not us_resident
    and not us_national
    and member.answers.writable_canada_mexico_resident
  consequence fulfilled

  # IRC 152(f)(1)(B). DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/childIsAdopted
  rule child_is_adopted under condition
    member.person.relationship = Q.Relationship.AdoptedChild
  consequence fulfilled

  # IRC 152(b)(3)(B); Treas. Reg. 1.152-2(a)(2)(i); Pub 501 p 11.
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/adoptedChildException
  rule adopted_child_exception under condition
    child_is_adopted
    and either_filer_us_citizen_or_national
    and member.person.residency_duration = Q.ResidencyDuration.AllYear
  consequence fulfilled

  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/citizenOrUsResidentTest
  rule citizen_or_us_resident_test under condition
    member.answers.is_us_citizen_full_year
    or us_resident
    or us_national
    or adopted_child_exception
  consequence fulfilled

  # IRC 152(b)(3); Pub 501 p 11. DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/citizenOrResidentTest
  rule citizen_or_resident_test under condition
    citizen_or_us_resident_test
    or canada_mexico_resident
    or adopted_child_exception
  consequence fulfilled
```

## Eligible to be claimed as a dependent

DF `/familyAndHousehold/*/eligibleDependentMinusFilerInformation` (line 2959)
is the `All` of: qualifying child or qualifying relative, not disqualified by
the divorced-parents rules (`disqualifiedDueToParentalCustody`, IRC 152(e);
issue #18), not the custodial side of a benefit split
(`tpCanClaimSplitBenefitsHohEitcCdcc`: the filer released the claim to the
other parent with Form 8332, IRC 152(e)(2); issue #18), the citizen or
resident test, the joint return test, and a TIN.

**The TIN.** IRC 152 does not ask for a TIN, but a dependent is claimed by
listing them on the return: Pub. 501 p 22, "You must show the SSN of any
dependent you list in the Dependents section of your Form 1040 or 1040-SR."
Pub. 501 p 23: "Alien or adoptee with no SSN. If your dependent doesn't have
and can't get an SSN, you must show the ITIN or adoption taxpayer
identification number (ATIN) instead of an SSN." See also IRC 151(e): "No
exemption shall be allowed under this section with respect to any individual
unless the TIN of such individual is included on the return claiming the
exemption." So a person with no SSN, ITIN or ATIN (`tinType` = `none`) is
not eligible to be claimed on this return. (A child born and died in 2024
with no SSN, which Pub. 501 p 23 allows with a birth certificate, is a DF
knockout, `/knockoutPersonBornAndDiedInTaxYearWithoutTin`, not modelled here.)

DF `/familyAndHousehold/*/eligibleDependent` (line 2987) adds IRC 152(b)(1):
not `/treatFilersAsDependents`. DF's description says it "does not account
for qualifying child tiebreakers"; `Qualifying_child` knocks those returns out
(DF-BUG-3).

```catala
scope DependentTest:
  # IRC 152(a), 152(b)(2), 152(b)(3), 152(e); Pub 501 pp 11, 22-23.
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/eligibleDependentMinusFilerInformation
  rule eligible_dependent_minus_filer_information under condition
    qc_or_qr
    and not member.answers.disqualified_due_to_parental_custody
    and not member.answers.tp_can_claim_split_benefits_hoh_eitc_cdcc
    and citizen_or_resident_test
    and qc.joint_return_test
    and member.answers.tin_type != NoTin
  consequence fulfilled

  # IRC 152(b)(1); Pub 501 p 11. DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/eligibleDependent
  rule eligible_dependent under condition
    not treat_filers_as_dependents
    and eligible_dependent_minus_filer_information
  consequence fulfilled
```

## Claimed dependents

A dependent counts on the return only if the filer claims them. DF
`/familyAndHousehold/*/isClaimedDependent` (line 3016): the filer answered
yes to claiming the person (`tpClaims`) and the person is eligible. DF
`/familyAndHousehold/*/isClaimedIfClaimable` (line 3423): the person is
claimed, or cannot be claimed. DF requires this of a qualifying person for
HoH, QSS, EITC and CDCC (its decision record "Must claim dependent on
return"); those modules (#19, #20, #26, #28) read it.

```catala
scope DependentTest:
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/isClaimedDependent
  rule is_claimed_dependent under condition
    member.answers.tp_claims and eligible_dependent
  consequence fulfilled

  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/isClaimedIfClaimable
  rule is_claimed_if_claimable under condition
    is_claimed_dependent or not eligible_dependent
  consequence fulfilled
```

## The household: every person

The scope `Dependents` applies `DependentTest` to each member of
`/familyAndHousehold`, in order, and counts the claimed dependents (DF
`/claimedDependentsCount`, `familyAndHousehold.xml` line 4720, the size of
`/claimedDependentsCollection`, line 4318).

```catala-metadata
declaration scope Dependents:
  # DF: /familyAndHousehold
  input members content list of Member
  # DF: filers.xml /minimumFilerDateOfBirth
  input minimum_filer_date_of_birth content date
  # DF: filers.xml /treatFilersAsDependents
  input treat_filers_as_dependents content boolean
  # DF: filers.xml /eitherFilerUSCitizenOrNational
  input either_filer_us_citizen_or_national content boolean

  # DF: /familyAndHousehold/*/... (one result per member, same order)
  output results content list of DependentTest
  # DF: /claimedDependentsCount
  output claimed_dependents_count content integer
```

```catala
scope Dependents:
  definition results equals
    map each m among members to
      (output of DependentTest with {
         -- member: m
         -- minimum_filer_date_of_birth: minimum_filer_date_of_birth
         -- treat_filers_as_dependents: treat_filers_as_dependents
         -- either_filer_us_citizen_or_national:
              either_filer_us_citizen_or_national })

  # DF: /claimedDependentsCount
  definition claimed_dependents_count equals
    number of (list of r among results such that r.is_claimed_dependent)
```
